# CODEOWNERS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Real-org extension pattern (not active in this 2-account demo environment):
# once further reviewers exist, hand /PFL/notebooks/ (day-to-day
# notebook code) to them directly - it's already split out below so this
# requires no restructuring, just changing the owner on one line.
# Prod-affecting paths (bundle target config, job resources, pipeline
# definitions) stay with a stricter/senior owner even after that split.
#
# Example with real teams in a larger org:
#   /databricks.yml         @org/prod-approvers
#   /PFL/resources/          @org/prod-approvers
#   /PFL/notebooks/           @org/qa-approvers
#   /.github/                 @org/platform-team
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Live demo state: 2 real accounts (stuti404, Stuti-Ganit), both listed as
# owners on every path. GitHub still structurally forbids self-approval, so
# whichever account authored a given PR can never satisfy this requirement
# with their own approval - the OTHER listed account always has to review it.
# This is why both names are listed everywhere rather than splitting paths
# between them: Stuti-Ganit authors most dev/uat PRs (stuti404 approves
# those), while stuti404 authors most promotion/admin PRs (Stuti-Ganit
# approves those) - listing both keeps either direction covered without
# ever silently reducing to a single approver. Matches the same two-account
# reviewer list already used on the production GitHub Environment's
# required-reviewer gate on publish-prod.

/databricks.yml       @stuti404 @Stuti-Ganit
/PFL/resources/       @stuti404 @Stuti-Ganit
/PFL/notebooks/       @stuti404 @Stuti-Ganit
/.github/             @stuti404 @Stuti-Ganit
